\begin_layout Title
Chapter 15: Binary Response Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.1 Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In binary response models, the variable to be explained, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, is a random variable taking on the values zero and one, which idicate
 whether or not a certian event has occured.
 Regardless of the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, it is traditional to refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 as a 
\emph on
success
\emph default
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 as a 
\emph on
failure
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the endogenous variable and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\equiv(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iK})$
\end_inset

 is the vector of explanatory variables.
 Interest lies primarily in the 
\series bold
response probability
\series default
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p(x_{i})\equiv P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=P(y_{i}=1|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iK})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for various values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a continuous variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on the response probability is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}=\frac{\partial p(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When multiplied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{ij}$
\end_inset

, equation (2) gives the approximate change in 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 increases by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 holding all other variables fixed (for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

small
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{ij}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, interest lies in
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{i,K-1},1)-p(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{i,K-1},0)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the difference in response probabilities when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}=0$
\end_inset

.
 For most of the models we consider, whether 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is continuous or discrete, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 depends on all of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=0|x_{i})=1-p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i})=p(x_{i})\left[1-p(x_{i})\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.2 Linear Probability Model for Binary Response
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
linear probability model (LPM)
\series default
 for binary response 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is specified as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{ik}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As usual, the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 can be functions of underlying explanatory variables, which would simply
 change the interpretations of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is not functionally related to the other explanatory variables, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the change in the probability of success given a one-unit increase in
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 Using functions such as quadratics, logarithms, and so on among the independent
 variables causes no new difficulties.
 The important point is that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 now measures the effects of the explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on a particular probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To obtain an appropriate estimation technique, it is useful to derive the
 conditional mean and variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a Bernoulli random variable, these are simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(y_{i}|x_{i}) & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{iK}\\
\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i}) & = & x_{i}^{\prime}\beta(1-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (5) implies that, given a random sample, the OLS regression of
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $1,x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 produces consistent and even unbiased estimators of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (6) means that heteroskedasticity is present unless all of the
 slope coefficients are zero.
 A nice way to deal with this issue is to use standard heteroskedasticity-robust
 standard errors and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics.
 Futher, robust tests of multiple restrictions should also be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we operate under the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is given by equation (4), then we can obtain an asymptotically more efficeint
 estimator by applying WLS.
 The usual standard errors from this regression are valid.
 However, if some of the OLS fitted values are not between zero and one,
 WLS analysis is not possible without ad hoc adjustments to bring deviant
 fitted values into the unit interval.
 Further, since the OLS fitted value 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an estimate of the conditional probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, it is somewhat awkward if the predicted probability is negative or above
 unity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Aside from the issue of fitted values being outside the unit interval, the
 LPM implies that a ceteris paribus unit increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 always changes 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 by the same amount, regardless of the initial value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 This feature of the LPM cannot literally be true because continually increasing
 one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 would eventaully drive 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be less than zero or greater than one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A sensible posture is to simply view the LPM as the linear projection of
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on the explanatory variables.
 If we operate within this scenario, OLS estimation of the LPM is attractive
 because it consistently estimates the parameters in the linear projection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the main purpose of estimating a binary response model is to approximate
 the partial effects of the explanatory variables, averaged scross the distribut
ion of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, then the LPM often does a very good job.
 The fact that some predicted probabilities are outside the unit interval
 need not be a serious concern.
 But there is no guarantee that the LPM provides good estimates of the partial
 effects for a wide range of covariate values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.3 Index Models for Binary Response: Probit and Logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now study binary responde models of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\equiv p(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 and we take the first element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be unity.
 For the LPM, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(z)=z$
\end_inset

 is the identity function, which means that the response probabilities cannot
 be between 0 and 1 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 In this section, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 take on values in the open unit interval: 
\begin_inset Formula $0<F(z)<1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The model in equation (7) is generally called an 
\series bold
index model
\series default
 because it restricts the way in which the response probabilities depends
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 only through the 
\emph on
index 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The function 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 maps the index into the response probability.
 In most applications, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a cumulative distribution function (cdf) whose specific form can sometimes
 be derived from an underlying economic model.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Index models where 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a cdf can be derived more generally from an underlying 
\series bold
latent variable model
\series default
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+e_{i};\ \ \ y_{i}=1\left[y_{i}^{*}>0\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a continuously distributed variable independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric about zero.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is the cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

, then, because the pdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is symmetrc about zero, 
\begin_inset Formula $1-G(-z)=G(z)$
\end_inset

 for all reall numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=P(y_{i}^{*}>0|x_{i})=P(e_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta|x_{i})=1-G(-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)=G(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\]

\end_inset

which is exactly equation (7).
 There is no particular reason for requiring 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be symmetrically distributed in the latent variable model, but this
 happens to be the case for the binary response models applied most often.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In most applications of binary response models, the primary goal is to explain
 the effects of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on the response probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The latent variable formulation tends to give the impression that we are
 primarily interested in the effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 As we will see, the 
\emph on
direction
\emph default
 of the effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{*}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

 are the same.
 The laten variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 rarely has a well-defined unit of measurement, so the magnitude of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is not especially meaningful except in special cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
probit model
\series default
 is the special case of equation (7) with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(z)=\Phi(z)\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{Z}}\phi(v)dv
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(z)$
\end_inset

 is the standard normal density
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi(z)=(2\pi)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\exp\left(-\nicefrac{z^{2}}{2}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The probit model can be derived from the latent variable formulation when
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a standard normal distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
logit model
\series default
 is a special case of equation (7) with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(z)=\Lambda(z)\equiv\nicefrac{\exp(z)}{\left[1+\exp(z)\right]}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This model arises from the latent variable forumalation when 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a standard logistic distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The general specification (7) allows us to cover probit, logit, and a number
 of other binary choice models in one framework.
 In fact, in what follows we do not even need 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 to be a cdf, but we do assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $F(z)$
\end_inset

 is strictly between zero and unity for all real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to successfully apply probit and logit models, it is important
 to know how to interpret the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 on both continuous and discrete explanatory variables.
 First, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial p(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}=f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\beta_{j},\ \mbox{where}\ f(z)\equiv\frac{dF}{d(z)}(z)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $p(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 through 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x_{i}\beta)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a strictly increasing cdf, as in the probit and logit cases, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the sign of the effect is given by the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, the 
\emph on
relative
\emph default
 effects do not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

: for continuous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ih}$
\end_inset

, the ration of the partial effects is constant and given by the ratio of
 the corresponding coefficients:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\nicefrac{\partial p(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}}{\nicefrac{\partial p(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ih}}}=\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\beta_{h}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In the typical case that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is a symmetric density about zero, with unique mode at zero, the largest
 effect is when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 is a binary explanatory variable, then the partial effect form changing
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 from zero to one, holding all other variables fixed is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\beta_{K})-F(\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Again, this expression depends on the other 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also use the difference expression (14) for other kinds of discrete
 variables.
 The effect on the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 going from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}+1$
\end_inset

 is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
F(\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\beta_{K}[c_{K}+1])\nonumber \\
-F(\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\beta_{K}c_{K})
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is also straightforward to include standard function forms among the
 explanatory variables.
 Models with interactions among explanatory variables, including interactions
 between discrete and continuous variables, are handled similarly.
 When measuring the effects of discrete variables, we should use expression
 (15).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.4 Maximum Likelihood Estimation of Binary Response Index Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 independent, identically distributed observations following the model (7).
 To estimate the model by (conditional) maximum likelihood (MLE), we need
 the log-likelihood function for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 The density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g(y_{i}|x_{i};\beta)=\left[F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]^{y_{i}}\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]^{1-y_{i}};\ \ \ y_{i}=0,1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The log-likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is a function of the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of parameters and the data 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\beta)=y_{i}\log\left[F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]+(1-y_{i})\log\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Restriction 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 to be strictly between zero and one ensures that 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\beta)$
\end_inset

 is well defined for all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As usual, the log-likehood for a sample size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\beta)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}l_{i}(\beta)$
\end_inset

, and the MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, maximizes this log-likelihood.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the standard normal cdf, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
probit estimator
\series default
; if 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the logistic cdf, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
logit
\series default
 
\series bold
estimator
\series default
.
 From the general maximum likelihood results we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is consistent and asymptotically normal.
 We can easily estimate the asymptotic variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We asusme that 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable, an assumption that is usually satisfied
 in applications.
 As before, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)$
\end_inset

 is the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(z)$
\end_inset

.
 For the probit model, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=\phi(z)$
\end_inset

, and for the logit model, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=\nicefrac{\exp(z)}{\left[1+\exp(z)\right]^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The score of the conditional log-likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
s_{i}(\beta) & = & y_{i}\frac{f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}-\frac{f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}+\frac{f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}y_{i}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{y_{i}f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]}-\frac{f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}\left[y_{i}-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Similarly, the expected value of the Hessian conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-E\left[H_{i}(\beta)|x_{i}\right]=\frac{\left[f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]}\equiv A(x_{i},\beta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 p.s.d.
 matrix for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i.$
\end_inset

 From the general conditional MLE results, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 is estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})}\equiv\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\frac{\left[f(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\right]^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}}{F(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\left[1-F(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\right]}\right\} ^{-1}\equiv\hat{V}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In most cases, the inverse exists, and when it does, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 is positive definite.
 If the matrix in equation (20) is not invertible, then perfect collinearity
 probably exists among the regressors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.5 Testing in Binary Response Index Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Any of the three tests form general MLE analysis, i.e., the Wald, likelihood
 ratio (LR), or Lagrange multiplier (LM) test, ca be used to test hypotheses
 in binary response contexts.
 Since the tests are all asymptotically equivalent under local alternatives,
 the choice of statistic usually depends on computational simplicity.
 Yet, there are some other testing situations that often arise in binary
 choice analysis where particular tests are recommended for their computational
 advantages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.6 Reporting the Results for Probit and Logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Usually, we want to estimate the effects of the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on the response probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is (roughly) continuous, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\Delta P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})}\approx\left[f(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\hat{\beta}_{j}\right]\Delta x_{ij}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for small changes in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the estimated partial effect of a continuous variable on the
 response probability, evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, we need to decide which partial effects to report.
 For summarizing the magnitudes of the effects, it is useful to have a single
 scale factor that can be used to multiply the coefficients on (roughly)
 continuous variables.
 Often, the sample averages of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are plugged in to get 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\bar{x}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{1}\equiv1$
\end_inset

 because we include a constant.
 We call the result partial effect the 
\series bold
partial effect at the average (PEA)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The PEA is easy to compute, but it does have drawbacks.
 First, it need not represent the partial effect for any particular unit
 in the population.
 That is, the average of the explanatory variables may not, in any sensible
 way, represent the average unit in the population.
 One issue is that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains nonlinear functions of underlying variables, we must decide whether
 to use the average of the nonlinear funciton or the nonlinear function
 of the average.
 If two or more elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are functionally related, it is no even clear what the PEAs of individual
 coefficients means.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For discrete variables, it is well known that the average need not even
 be a possible outcome of the variable.
 If, say, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the fraction of ones in the sample, and therefore cannot correspond
 to a particular unit (which must have zero or one).
 One way to overcome this conceptual problem is to compute partial effects
 separately for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=0$
\end_inset

, but then we no longer have a single number to report as the partial effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To obtain standard errors of the partial effects in equation (21), we can
 use the delta method.
 Consider the case 
\begin_inset Formula $j=K$
\end_inset

 for notational simplicity, and for given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, define 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{K}=\beta_{K}f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)=\nicefrac{\partial P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ik}}$
\end_inset

.
 Write this relation as 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{K}=h(\beta)$
\end_inset

 to denot that this a (nonlinear) function of the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The gradient of 
\begin_inset Formula $h(b)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{\beta}h(\beta)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{K}\frac{df}{dz}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\\
\beta_{K}x_{2}\frac{df}{dz}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\\
\vdots\\
\beta_{K}x_{K-1}\frac{df}{dz}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\\
\beta_{K}x_{K}\frac{df}{dz}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)+f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{df}{dz}$
\end_inset

 is simply the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 with respect to its argument.
 The delta method implies that the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 is estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\nabla_{\beta}h(\hat{\beta})\right]^{\prime}\hat{V}\left[\nabla h(\hat{\beta})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 is the asymptotic variance estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 The asymptotic standard error of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 is simply the square root of expression (22).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is a discrete variable, then we can estimate the change in the predicted
 probability in going from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}+1$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\delta}_{K} & = & F\left[\hat{\beta}_{1}+\hat{\beta}\bar{x}_{2}+\cdots+\hat{\beta}_{K-1}\bar{x}_{K-1}+\hat{\beta}_{K}(c_{K}+1)\right]\nonumber \\
 &  & -F\left(\hat{\beta}_{1}+\hat{\beta}\bar{x}_{2}+\cdots+\hat{\beta}_{K-1}\bar{x}_{K-1}+\hat{\beta}_{K}c_{K}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

In particular, when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, set 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Of course, the other variables can be evaluated anywhere, but the use of
 sample averages is typical.
 The delta method can be used to obtain a standard error of equation (23).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An alternative way to summarize the estimated marginal effects is to estimate
 the average value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

 across the population, or 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}E\left[g(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This quantity is the 
\series bold
average partial effect (APE)
\series default
.
 Here we are averaging across the distribution of all observable covariates.
 A consistent estimator of the APE is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{K}\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}f(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is continuous or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left[F\left(\hat{\beta}_{1}+\hat{\beta}x_{i,2}+\cdots+\hat{\beta}_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\hat{\beta}_{K}\right)-F\left(\hat{\beta}_{1}+\hat{\beta}x_{i,2}+\cdots+\hat{\beta}_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is binary.
 Naturally, we can use equation (25) to estimate the APE from changing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 (continuous or discrete) from any two values, say 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}^{0}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{K}^{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 is replaced with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}c_{K}^{1}$
\end_inset

 in the first term and we insert 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}c_{K}^{0}$
\end_inset

 intwo the second occurence of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 Further, in either equation (24) or (25), we can fix some of the explanatory
 variables at specific values and average across the remaining ones.
 As in the case of the PEA, if some elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are functions of each other, obtaining APEs of the form in equation (24)
 is not especially useful.
 We can obtain standard errors of the estimators in (24) and (25) by applying
 the boostrap to an average.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Generally, we should not expect the scale factors for the PEAs and APEs
 to be similar.
 That is, we should not expect 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\bar{x}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 to be similar to 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}g(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

.
 The reason is simple: the average of a nonlinear function (APE) is not
 the nonlinear function of the average (PEA).
 In the population, the scales are not the same either because the expected
 value do not pass through nonlinear functions: 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left[E(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]\neq E\left[f(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unline the magnitudes of the coefficient estiamtes, the APEs (and, to a
 lesser extent, the PEAs) can be compared across models.
 Remember, logit, probit, and the LPM implicitly use different scale factos.
 The scale for the LMP is unity.
 For logit and probit, we can look at the functions 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 at zero to obtain a rough idea of how the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

, may differ.
 As a general rule, the estimates from an LPM estiamtion are more comparable
 to the APEs than to the PEAs.
 As mentioned before, the PEAs need not come clos to representing an individual
 in the population.
 On the other hand, the APE is the partial effect averaged across the population
 and, in one case, we can show that the LPM estimates are consistent for
 the APEs 
\emph on
regardless of the actual function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.7
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Specificaion Issues in Binary Response Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to several issues that can arise in applying binary response
 models to economic data.
 All of these topics are relevant for general index models, but features
 of the normal distribution allos us to obtain concrete results in the context
 of probit models.
 Therefore, our primary focus is on probit models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.7.1 Neglected Heterogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We begin by studying the consequences of omitting variables when those omitted
 variables are 
\emph on
independent
\emph default
 of the included explanatory variables.
 This is also called the 
\series bold
neglected hterogeneity
\series default
 problem.
 The (structural) model of interest is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i},c)=\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma c)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}\equiv1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is a scalar.
 We are interested in the partial effects of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 on the probability of succes, holding 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 (and the other elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

) fixed.
 We can write equation (26) in latent varible form as 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma c+e_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1\left[y^{*}>0\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}|x_{i},c\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}\equiv1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c)=0$
\end_inset

 without loss of generality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\sim N(0,\tau^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Under independence the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Given these assumptions, the composite term, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma c+e_{i}$
\end_inset

, is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and has a 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\gamma^{2}\tau^{2}+1)$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=P(\gamma c+e_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta|x_{i})=\Phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}\equiv\gamma^{2}\tau^{2}+1$
\end_inset

.
 It follows immediately from equation (27) that probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
 In othe words, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the estimator from a probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p}\lim\hat{\beta}_{j}=\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=(\gamma^{2}\tau^{2}+1)^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}>1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sigma}\right|<\left|\beta_{j}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 This is known as 
\series bold
attenuation bias
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The probit coefficients are inconsistent since probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 However, the scaled coefficient, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

, has the same sign as 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

, and so we will correctly (with enough data) determine the direction of
 the partial effect of any variable by estimating the scaled coefficients.
 Further, the ratio of any two scaled coefficients, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{h}}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

, is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\beta_{h}}$
\end_inset

.
 For a continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

, the partial effect in model (26), which we call the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

structural partial effect,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial P(y_{i}=1|x_{i},c)}{\partial x_{ij}}=\beta_{j}\phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+c)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, the ratio of partial effects for two continuous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\beta_{h}}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, by averaging out the unboserved heterogeneity, we are left with
 the APE, which is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, the APE is obtained, for given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, by averaging equation (28) across the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 in the population.
 When we plug in a given value of the explanatory variables, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{o}$
\end_inset

, into equation (28) and take the expected value with respect to the distributio
n of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[\beta_{j}\phi(x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma c)\right]=\left(\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sigma}\right)\phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{o}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)
\]

\end_inset

In other words, probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 consistently estimates the APE.
 Note that since 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, then partial effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are always the average partial effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i},c)$
\end_inset

 over the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
 The results presented in this section do not hold if 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is correlated with or is otherwise dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 because we no longer consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 or is otherwise dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 (for example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

), the omission of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is serious.
 To illustrate, consider the lienar projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\delta+e_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\eta^{2})
\]

\end_inset

So, we can rewrite the latent variable as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i}^{*} & = & x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma c_{i}+u_{i}\\
y_{i}^{*} & = & x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta+e_{i})+u_{i}\\
y_{i}^{*} & = & x_{i}^{\prime}(\gamma+\beta)+\gamma e_{i}+u_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\gamma e_{i}+u_{i}\sim N(0,1+\gamma^{2}\eta^{2})
\]

\end_inset

and the probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}^{*}>0|x_{i})\\
 & = & P(\gamma e_{i}+u_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}(\gamma+\beta)|x_{i})\\
 & = & P\left(\left.\frac{\gamma e_{i}+u_{i}}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^{2}\eta^{2}}}<\frac{x_{i}^{\prime}(\gamma+\beta)}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^{2}\eta^{2}}}\right|x_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{x_{i}^{\prime}(\gamma+\beta)}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^{2}\eta^{2}}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which gives consistent estimates of
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\gamma+\beta}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^{2}\eta^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

In this case, we cannot get consistent estimates of the estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 We now study what can be done to account for endogenous variables in probit
 models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.7.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuous Endogenous Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now explicitly allow for the case where one of the explanatory variables
 is correlated with the error term in the latent variable model.
 If we want to estimate a probit model with endogenous explanatory variables,
 we must make some fairly strong assumptions.
 In this section, we consider the case of a continuous endogenous explanatory
 variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Write the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{1i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+u_{i}\\
y_{2i} & = & z_{1u}^{\prime}\gamma_{1}+z_{2i}^{\prime}\gamma_{2}+v_{i}=z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i}\\
y_{1i} & = & 1[y_{1i}^{*}>0]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 has zero mean, bivariate normal distribution, and is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (29), along with equation (31), is the structural equation; equation
 (3) is a reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, which is endogenous if 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are independent, then there is no endogeneity problem.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is normally distributed, we are assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is normal; thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 should have freatures of a normal random variable.
 The normalization that gives the parameters in equation (29) an average
 partial effect interpretation, at least in the context of endogeneity,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i})=1$
\end_inset

, just as in a probit model with all explanatory variables exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The most useful two-step approach is a control function approach as it leads
 to a simple test for endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 To derive the procedure, first note that, under joint normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i})=1$
\end_inset

, we can write the linear project of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}=\theta v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\nicefrac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}},$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{uv}=\mbox{Cov}(v_{i},u_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=\mbox{Var}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Because of joint normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is also normally distributed with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(e_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(e_{i})=\mbox{Var}(u_{i})-\nicefrac{\sigma_{uv}^{2}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}=1-\rho^{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\mbox{Corr}(v_{i},u_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 We can now write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{1i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+\theta v_{i}+e_{i}\\
D(e_{i}|z_{i},y_{2i},v_{i})=D(e_{i}|z_{i},v_{i})=D(e_{i}|z_{i}) & = & N(0,1-\rho^{2})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

A standard calculation shows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{1i}=1|x_{i},y_{2i},v_{i})=\Phi\left[\left(\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+\theta v_{i}}{\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Assuming for the moment that we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho1}\equiv\nicefrac{\beta_{1}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho2}\equiv\nicefrac{\beta_{2}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\rho}\equiv\nicefrac{\theta}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that because 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho^{2}<1$
\end_inset

, each scaled coefficient is greater than its unscaled counterpart unless
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rho=0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since we do not know 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, we must first estimate it, as in the following procedure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*
(a) Run the OLS regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and save the residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*
(b) Run the probit 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to get consistent estimators of the scaled coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\rho}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A nice feature of this algorthim is that the usual probit 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a valid test of the null hypothesis that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, that is 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\theta=0$
\end_inset

.
 Under this null hypothesis, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=u_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 plays no role.
 Therefore, the 
\emph on
test
\emph default
 of exogeneity is valid without assuming normality or homoskedasticity of
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, and it can be implied very broadly.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\neq0$
\end_inset

, then the normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is crucial.
 Also, the usual probit standard errors and test statistics are not striclty
 valid since we have only estimated 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 up to scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After the two-step estimation, we can easily obtain estimates of the unscaled
 parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

, which then allows us to estimate partial effects.
 Moreover, the asymptotic variance of the two-step estimator can be derived
 using the M-estimator results.
 From the two-step estimation procedure, we have consistent estimators of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the first-stage regression, and then 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{\rho2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\rho}$
\end_inset

 from the second stage.
 Straightforward algebra shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\nicefrac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}=\nicefrac{\rho}{\sigma_{v}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\rho}\equiv\nicefrac{\theta}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}=\nicefrac{\rho}{\sigma_{v}\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}$
\end_inset

, which we use to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
 Now, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}=\beta_{\rho1}\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}=\beta_{\rho_{2}}\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}$
\end_inset

, we can obtain consistent estimators of the original coefficients
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{1} & = & \beta_{\rho1}\sqrt{(1-\hat{\rho}^{2})}\\
\hat{\beta}_{2} & = & \beta_{\rho2}\sqrt{(1-\hat{\rho}^{2})}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where all quantities on the right-hand side of the equations are available
 from the two-step estimation procedure and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}$
\end_inset

 is the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
 However, with the CF approach, getting the appropriate standard errors
 is difficult.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rather than use a two-step procedure, we can estimate equations (29)-(31)
 by CLME.
 To obtain the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},y_{i2})$
\end_inset

, conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, recall that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(y_{1i},y_{2i}|z_{i})=f(y_{1i}|y_{2i},z_{i})\cdot f(y_{2i}|x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}|z_{i}\sim N(z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{v}^{2})$
\end_inset

, then density of 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i2}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is easy to write down.
 We can also derive the conditional density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i2},z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}=1[y_{1i}^{*}>0]$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{1i}=1|y_{2i},z_{i})=\Phi\left[\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+(\nicefrac{\rho}{\sigma_{v}})(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we have used the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\nicefrac{\rho}{\sigma_{v}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 denote the term inside 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 in equation (38).
 Then we have derived
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{1i},y_{2i}|z_{i})=\left\{ \Phi(w)\right\} ^{y_{i1}}\left\{ 1-\Phi(w)\right\} ^{1-y_{1i}}(\nicefrac{1}{\sigma_{v}})\phi\left[\nicefrac{(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}{\sigma_{v}}\right]
\]

\end_inset

and so the log likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (apart from terms not depending on the parameters) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}\log\Phi(w_{i})+(1-y_{i1i})\log[1-\Phi(w_{i})]-\frac{1}{2}\log(\sigma_{v}^{2})-\frac{1}{2}\nicefrac{(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)^{2}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we understand that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 depends on the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\rho,\gamma,\sigma_{v})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{i}\equiv\frac{\left[z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+(\nicefrac{\rho}{\sigma_{v}})(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)\right]}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}
\]

\end_inset

Summing expression (39) across all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and maximizing with respect to all parameters gives the MLEs of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The general theory of conditional MLE applies, and so standard errors can
 be obtained using the estimated Hessian, the estimated expected Hessian,
 or the outer produce of the score.
 MLE applied to this model sometimes goes by the name of 
\series bold
instrumental variables probit
\series default
, or 
\series bold
IV
\series default
 
\series bold
probit
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Maximum likelihood estimation has some decided advantages over two-step
 procedures.
 First, MLE is more efficient than any two-step procedure.
 Second, we get direct estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

, the parameters of interest for computing partial effects.
 Testing that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous is easy once the MLE has been obtained: just test 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\rho=0$
\end_inset

 using an asymptotic 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 test.
 We could also use a likelihood ratio test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another benefit of the MLE approach for this and related problems is that
 it forces discipline on us in coming up with consistent estimation procedures
 and correct standard errors.
 It is easy to abuse two-step procedures if we are not careful in derviing
 estimating equations.
 With MLE, although it can be difficult to derive joint distributions of
 the endogenous variables given the exogenous variables, we know that, if
 the underlying distributional assumptions hold, consistent and efficient
 estimators are obtained.
 The drawback to the MLE is computational.
 Sometimes it can be difficult to et the iterations to converge, as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}$
\end_inset

 sometimes tends to 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The CF approach has a somewhat subtle robustness property.
 It is easy to see that the CF approach is consistent if we assume that
 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|z_{i},v_{i})=D(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 and that 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is normal with mean linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 and constant variance.
 Independence between 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 along with bivariate normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is sufficient but not necessary.
 It is certainly possible for 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be normal without 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 having a normal distribution.
 In fact, we could even allow the mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be a known, nonlinear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 and then add these functions to the probit in the second-step estimation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another nice feature of the CF approach is that we can allow for a strictly
 monotonic transformation of the endogenous explanatory variable in the
 reduced form.
 That including the reduced form residuals in the CF approach accounts for
 endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

, even if we have general functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

 in the model, hinges crucially on independence between 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Comparing the CF approach to the MLE shows that the former is a 
\series bold
limited information procedure
\series default
.
 However, the two-step estimators and MLEs are identical when the model
 is just identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we have multiple endogenous explanatory variables, say a 
\begin_inset Formula $G\times1$
\end_inset

 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, we can solve the endogeneity problem in a very similar way.
 MLE becomes much more difficult computationally, but the CF approach is
 straightforward.
 The key assumption for the CF approach is 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Gamma+v_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|z_{i},v_{i})=D(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 and this latter distribution is normal with constant variance and mean
 linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 MLE is also feasible when 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is jointly normal and independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.7.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Binary Endogenous Explanatory Variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider the case where the probit model contains a 
\emph on
binary
\emph default
 explanatory variable that is endogenous.
 We begin with the simplest model,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{i1} & = & 1\left[z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+u_{i}>0\right]\\
y_{i2} & = & 1\left[z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i}>0\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and distributed as bivariate normal with mean zero each has unit variance,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\mbox{Cov}(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated, and probit estimation of (40) is inconsistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Model (40) and (41) applies primarily to omitted variables situations.
 In particular, we could not obtain the reduced form (41) if the structural
 model has a 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}$
\end_inset

 as a determinant of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 Measurement error in binary responses does not lead to this model, either.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As discussed in Section 15.7.2, the normalization 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 is the proper one for computing partial effects.
 Often, the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is of primary interest, especially when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}$
\end_inset

 indicates participation in some sort of program, and the binary outcome
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}$
\end_inset

 might denote employment status.
 The average treatment effect (for a given value of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(z_{i1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\beta_{2})-\Phi(z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1})$
\end_inset

.
 This effect can be computed for different subgroups or averaged across
 the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To derive the likelihood funciton, we again need the joint distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, which we obtain from equation (37).
 To obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i1}=1|y_{2i},z_{i})$
\end_inset

, first note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{1i}=1|v_{i},z_{i})=\Phi\left[\nicefrac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+\rho v_{1}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}=1$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

, we need a basic fact about truncated normal distributions: If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a standard normal distribution and is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, then the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\phi(v_{i})}{P(v_{i}>-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}=\frac{\phi(v_{i})}{\Phi(z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(y_{1i}=1|y_{2i}=1,z_{i}) & = & E\left[P(y_{1i}=1|v_{i},z_{i})|y_{2i}=1,z_{i}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left\{ \left.\Phi\left[\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{i2i}\beta_{2}+\rho v_{i}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}\right]\right|y_{2i}=1,z_{i}\right\} \nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{1}{\Phi(z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}{\displaystyle \intop_{-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}^{\infty}}\Phi\left[\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+\rho v_{i}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}\right]\phi(\mathscr{V}_{i})d\mathscr{V}_{i}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

were 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{V}_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the integral is a dummy argument of integration.
 Of course, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{1i}=0|y_{2i}=1,z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is just one minus equation (44).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Similarly, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{1i}=1|y_{1i}=0,z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{1-\Phi(z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)}{\displaystyle \intop_{-z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}^{\infty}}\Phi\left[\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+y_{2i}\beta_{2}+\rho v_{i}}{\sqrt{(1-\rho^{2})}}\right]\phi(\mathscr{V}_{i})d\mathscr{V}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Combining the four possible outcomes of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

, along with the probit model for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and taking the 
\begin_inset Formula $\log$
\end_inset

 gives the log-likelihood funciton for MLE analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.7.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heteroskedasticity and Nonnormality in the Latent Variable Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In applying the probit model, it is easy to become confused about the problems
 of heteroskedasticity and nonnormality.
 The confusion stems from a failure to distinguish between the underlying
 latent variable formulation, as in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+e_{i}$
\end_inset

, and the response probability in equation (7).
 As we have emphasized, for most purposes we want to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The latent variable formulation is convenient for certain manipulations,
 but we are rarely interested in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y^{*}|x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First consider the problem of nonnormality of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 in a probit model.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=G(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\neq\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

, where, generally, 
\begin_inset Formula $G(z)=1-F(-z)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 While nonnormality in a probit model causes bias and inconsistency in the
 parameter estimates, we can still consistently estimate the APEs.
 The probit model is likely to do a reasonable job of approximating the
 APEs in lots of cases where 
\begin_inset Formula $G\neq\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Nonnormality in 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1[x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+e_{i}>0]$
\end_inset

 means that the probit response probability is incorrect.
 Even if we could estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 consistently we could not obtain magnitutes of the partial effects.
 So our focus should be on how well different methods approximate partial
 effects, and not on whether they estimate parameters consistently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Similar comments can be made about heteroskedasticity in the latent error
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $D(e_{i}|x_{i})=N(0,h(x))$
\end_inset

, the response probability takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i1}=1|x_{i})=\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sqrt{h(x)}}\right)$
\end_inset

, and so it is pretty obvious that probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 could not consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Even if the usual probit model is correct, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(e_{i}|x_{i})=1$
\end_inset

, the variance of the response variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, is heteroskedastic: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i})=\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\{1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Is there any reason we should consider possible heteroskedasticity in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}^{*}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an unknowable latent variable? There are two reasons.
 The simplest is for generalizing the functional form of the response probabilit
y, just as when we allow for nonnormality in 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}^{*}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Consider a probit model where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(e_{i}|x_{i})=\exp(2x_{i1}^{\prime}\delta)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})=\Phi\left[\exp(-x_{i1}^{\prime}\delta)x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right]$
\end_inset

, and so we have potentially allowed a much more flexible response probability.
 But there is a cost to the more general model: the partial effects, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}$
\end_inset

 are more complicated.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the coefficient on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 in the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial P(y_{i}=1|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}=\phi\left[\exp(-x_{i1}^{\prime}\delta)x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right]\exp(-x_{i1}^{\prime}\delta)\left[\beta_{j}-\delta_{j}\cdot(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so the sign of the partial effect at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 depends on the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}-\delta_{j}\cdot(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

; it need not be the same sign as 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 These days, estimating a so-called 
\series bold
heteroskedastic probit model
\series default
 (which usually means an exponential conditional variance for 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

) is fairly straightforward.
 The challenge is in intelligently using the resulting estimates.
 One possibility is to compute partial effects from equation (46) and average
 them across the sample.
 In any case, it makes no sense to compare the estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 from a heteroskedastic probit with those from a standard probit.
 In each case the MLE estiamtes adjust to fit the data, and this often means
 the different methods can provide similar partial effects, at least for
 some values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is another, more subtle reason to entertain the notion of heteroskedastici
ty in the latent variable model.
 The partial effects in the model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=1[x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+e_{i}>0]$
\end_inset

, averaged across the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
are
\emph default
 the same sign as the corresponding 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, the ratios of APEs for the continuous variables are equal to the
 ratios of the coefficients, even when 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
15.8
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Binary Response Models for Panel Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When analyzing binary responses in the context of pnael data, it is often
 useful to begin with a linear model with an additive, unobserved effect,
 and then use the within transformation or first differencing to remove
 the unobserved effect.
 A linear probability model for binary outcomes has the same problems as
 in the cross section case.
 In fact, it is probably less appealing for unobserved effects models, as
 it implies the unnatral restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}^{\prime}\beta\leq c_{i}\leq1-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 To see this, note that the response probability for an unobserved effects
 LPM is 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{it}=1|x_{i},c_{i})=P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 As in the pure cross section case, the linear functional form is almost
 certainly false, except in special cases.
 But FE or FD estimation of the LPM might provide reasonable estimates of
 APEs.
 Plus, the LPM has the advantage of not requiring a distribution assumption
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $D(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 (which means we do not need to be concerned that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is bounded by linear combinations of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

); nor do we have to assume independence of the responses 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 (provided we make our inference robust to serial dependence, as well as
 heteroskedasticity).
 The nonlinear methods we cover in this seciton require one or both of these
 assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.8.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pooled Probit and Logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose the model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it})=G(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta),\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $G(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a known function taking on values in the open unit interval.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 can contain a variety of factors, including time dummies, interactions
 of time dummies with time-constant or time-varying variables, and lagged
 dependent variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In specifying the model (47) we have not assumed nearly enough to obtain
 the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}\equiv(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=(x_{i1}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{iT}^{\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, for two reasons.
 First, we have not assumed 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})=D(y_{it}|x_{it})$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 is not necessarily strictly exogenous.
 Second, even if we assume strict exogeneity, we have not resticted the
 dependence in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 As with many pooled MLE problems, we have only specified a model for 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{it})$
\end_inset

.
 If that model is correctly specified, we can obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent, asymptotically normal estimator by maximizing the partial (pooled)
 log-likelihood funciton.
 For binary response, we maximize the partial log-likelihood
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\left\{ y_{it}\log G(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta)+(1-y_{it})\log\left[1-G(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta)\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

which is simply an exercise in pooled estimation.
 Without further assumptions, a robust variance matrix estimator is needed
 to account for serial correlation in the scores across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case that the model (47) is dynamically complete, that is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},y_{i,t-1},x_{i,t-1},\ldots)=P(y_{it}=1|x_{it})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D(y_{it}|x_{it},y_{i,t-1},x_{i,t-1},\ldots)=D(y_{it}|x_{it})
\]

\end_inset

inference is considerably easier: all the usual statistics from a probit
 or logit that pools observations and treats the sample as a long independent
 cross section of size 
\begin_inset Formula $NT$
\end_inset

 are valid, including likelihood ratio statistics.
 Remember, we are definitely 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 assuming independence across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 For example, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 can contain lagged dependent variables.
 Dynamic completeness implies that the scores are serially uncorrelated
 across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, which is the key condition for the standard inference procedures to be
 valid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.8.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unobserved Effects Probit Models Under Strict Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A popular model for binary outcomes with panel data is the 
\series bold
unobserved effects probit model
\series default
.
 A key assumption for many of the estimators for unobserved effects probit
 models, as well as other nonlinear panel data models, is that the observed
 covariates are 
\series bold
strictly exogenous conditional on the unoberved effect
\series default
.
 Now, we need this assumption in terms of conditional distributions, which
 we can state generically as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})\equiv D(y_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=D(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i}),\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the unobserved effect and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\equiv(x_{i1}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{iT}^{\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 In stating this asusmption, we are not restricting in any way the joint
 distribution conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

, which we write as 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption (49) rules out models with lagged dependent variables in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 and also other situations where one or more elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 may react in the future to idiosyncratic changes in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption (49) also requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 includes enough lags of underlying explanatory variables if distributed
 lag dyanmics are present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the unobserved effects probit model, we specify a response probability
 that fully determines the conditional distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})=\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}),\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Most analyses are not convincing unless 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 contains a full set of time dummies.
 In what follows, we usually leave time dummies implicit to simplify the
 notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our interest is in the response probability (50), and we would like to be
 able to estimate the parameters and partial effects just by specifying
 equation (50).
 However, just specifying a model with contemporaneous conditioning variables
 is not sufficient for estimating parameters; without more assumptions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is not identified.
 Even adding the strict exogeneity assumption (49) is not enough.
 We must specify how 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 relates to the covariates.
 One possibility is to add, in addition to assumptions (49) and (50), the
 asusmption that the responses are independent conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}\ \mbox{are independent conditional on}\ (x_{i},c_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Important to note, treating the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as parameters to estimate can lead to potentially serious biases, as in
 the probit case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here we follow the same approach adopted for linear models: we 
\emph on
always
\emph default
 treat 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

as an unobservable random variable drawn along with 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 For the unobserved effects probit model, we must make an assumption about
 the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
traditional random effects probit model
\series default
 adds to assumptions (49), (50), and (51), the assumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{c}^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is a strong assumption, as it implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are independent and that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a normal distribution.
 It is not enough to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated, or even that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 The assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 is without loss of generality provided 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 contains an intercept, as it always should.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consistent estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 means that we can consistently estimate the partial effects of the elements
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on the response probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 at the average value of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the population, 
\begin_inset Formula $c=0$
\end_inset

.
 Also, APEs are at least as useful.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{c}^{2})$
\end_inset

, the APE for a continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{itj}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}}}\right]\phi\left[\nicefrac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta}{\sqrt{(1+\sigma_{c}^{2})}}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we only need to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{c}\equiv\nicefrac{\beta}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}}}$
\end_inset

 to estimate the APEs, for either continuous or discrete explanatory variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under assumptions (49), (50), (51), and (52), a conditional maximum likelihood
 approach is available for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are not observed, they cannot appear in the likelihood function.
 Instead, we find the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 , a step that requires us to 
\emph on
integrate out 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{c}^{2})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i};\theta)={\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}\left[{\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{T}}f(y_{it}|x_{it},c;\beta)\right](\nicefrac{1}{\sigma_{c}})\phi(\nicefrac{c}{\sigma_{c}})d\mathscr{C}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i};\beta)=\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c)^{y_{it}}\left[1-\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c)\right]^{1-y_{it}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 contain 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and taking the log of equation (53) gives the conditional log likelihood
 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 The log-likelihood funciton for the entire sample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 can be maximized with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent asymptotically normal estimators.
 The CLME in this context is typically called the 
\series bold
random effects probit estimator
\series default
.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be estimated, the partial effects at 
\begin_inset Formula $c=0$
\end_inset

 as well as the APEs can be estimated.
 Since the variance of the idiosyncratic error in the latent variable model
 is unity, the relative importance of the unobserved effect is measured
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\nicefrac{\sigma_{c}^{2}}{(\sigma_{c}^{2}+1)}$
\end_inset

, which is also the correlation between the composite latent error, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}+e_{it}$
\end_inset

, across any two time periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As in the linear case, in many applications the point of introducing the
 unobserved effect, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, is to explicitly allow unobservables to be correlated with some elements
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 We allow for correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 by assuming a conditional normal distribution with linear expectation and
 constant variance.
 A version of this assumption is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{a}^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the average of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the equation 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=\psi+\bar{x}_{i}\gamma+a_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 (In other words, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the conditional variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is assumed not to depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 Model (50) under asusmptions (49) and (54) is known as a 
\series bold
correlated random effects probit model
\series default
.
 While assumption (54) is restrictive in that it specifies a distirbution
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, it at least allows for some dependence between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As in the linear case, we can only estimate the effects of time-varying
 elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 should no longer contain a constant, as that would be indistinguishable
 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 in assumption (54).
 If our originial model contians a time-constant explanatory variable, say
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, it can be included among the explanatory variables, but we cannot distinguish
 its effect from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 unless we assume that the coefficient for 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is zero.
 (That is, unless we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is partially uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

.) Time dummies, whcih do not vary across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, are omitted from 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If assumptions (49), (50), (51), and (54) hold, estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is straightforward because we can write the latent variable as 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}^{*}=\psi+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}+e_{it}$
\end_inset

, where the 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{it}$
\end_inset

 are independent Normal(0,1) variates (conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},a_{i})$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{a}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, by adding 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to the equation for each time period, we arrive at a traditional RE model.
 (The variance we estimate is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

, but this suits are purposes nicely.) Adding 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 as a set of controls for unobserved heterogeneity is very intuitive: we
 are estimating the effect of changing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{itj}$
\end_inset

 but holding the time average fixed.
 A test of the usual RE probit model is easily obtained as a test of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\gamma=0$
\end_inset

.
 Estimation can be carried out using standard RE probit software.
 Given estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, we can estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=\psi+E(\bar{x}_{i})\gamma$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{c}\equiv\hat{\psi}+\bar{x}^{\prime}\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 is the sample average of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, for any vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, we can estimate the response probability at 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\hat{\mu}_{c}+x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

.
 Taking differences or derivatives (with respect to the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

) allows us to estimate the partial effects on the response probabilities
 for any value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Further, we can estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 by using the relationship 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}=\gamma^{\prime}\mbox{Var}(\bar{x}_{i})\gamma+\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

, and so 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}\equiv\hat{\gamma}^{\prime}\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left(\bar{x}_{i}-\bar{x}\right)^{\prime}\left(\bar{x}_{i}-\bar{x}\right)\right]\hat{\gamma}+\hat{\sigma}_{a}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

 is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we can plug in values of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 that are a certain number of estimated standard deviations fomr 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{c}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not generally have an unconditional normal distribution, unless 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

 is normally distributed; sufficient is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is multivariate normal.
 With large 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 and weak dependence in the time series dimension, it might be that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

 has an approximate normal distribution (because it is an average across
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

).
 but weak dependence is not necessarily a good assumption, and even if it
 were, if we had a large 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 we might proceed by treating the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

as parameters to estimate.
 Generally, we should not epect the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be normal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we drop assumption (51), we can still estimate scaled versions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

.
 Under assumptions (49), (50), and (54) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{i}) & = & \Phi\left[\left(\psi+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\cdot\left(1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\right)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\right]\nonumber \\
 & \equiv & \Phi\left(\psi_{a}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{a}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where the 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 subscript means that a parameter vector has been multiplied by 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\sigma_{a}^{2})^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 It follows immediately that 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{a}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{a}$
\end_inset

 can be consistently estimated using a pooled probit analysis of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T,\ i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 Because the 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 will be dependent condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, inference that is robust to arbitrary time dependence is required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Conveniently, once we have esitmated 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{a}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{a}$
\end_inset

 we can estimate the APEs.
 To see how, we need to average 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{t}=1|x_{t}=x_{o},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 across the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

; that is, we need to find 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[P(y_{t}=1|x_{t}=x_{o},c_{i})\right]=E\left[\Phi(x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]$
\end_inset

 for any given value 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{o}$
\end_inset

 of the explanatory variables.
 (In what follows, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{o}$
\end_inset

 is a nonrandom vector of numbers that we choose as interesting values of
 the explanatory variables.
 For emphasis, we include an 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 subscript on the random variables appearing in the expectations.) Writing
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}$
\end_inset

 and using iterated expectations, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\Phi\left(\psi+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta+a_{i}\right)\right]=E\left[E\left\{ \Phi\left(\psi+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}\right)|x_{i}\right\} \right]$
\end_inset

 [where the first expectation is with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},a_{i})$
\end_inset

].
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\Phi\left(\psi+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}\right)|x_{i}\right]=\Phi\left[\left\{ \psi+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right\} \cdot\left(1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\right)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\right]=\Phi\left(\psi_{a}+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{a}\right)$
\end_inset

 and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\Phi\left(x_{o}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}\right)\right]=E\left[\Phi\left(\psi_{a}+x_{o}^{\prime}\beta_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{a}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because the only rnadom variable in the right-hand-side expectation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

, a consistent estimator of the right-hand side equation of (56) is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Phi\left(\hat{\psi}_{a}+x_{o}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\gamma}_{a}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

APEs can be estimated by evaluating expression (57) at two different values
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{o}$
\end_inset

 and forming the difference, or, for continous variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

, bu using the average across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{aj}\phi\left(\hat{\psi}_{a}+x_{o}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\gamma}_{a}\right)$
\end_inset

 to get the approximate APE of a one-unit increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
15.8.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unobserved Effects Logit Models Under Strict Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The unobserved effects probit model of the previous subset has a logit counterpa
rt.
 In the leading case, the heterogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, is still assumed to satisfy (52), but we replace (50) with the response
 probability
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})=\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}),\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the logistic function.
 If we maintain the full set of assumptions, given by the strict exogeneity
 condition (49), the conditional independence assumption (51), the normality
 assumption (52), and the model in equation (58), we arrive at the 
\series bold
random effects logit model
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From a computational standpoint, the RE logit model is less desirable than
 the RE probit model.
 Even if we focus on a pooled method under assumptions (52) and (58), estimation
 is complicated because the response probability obtained by integrating
 out 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it})={\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})(\nicefrac{1}{\sigma_{c}})\phi(\nicefrac{c}{\sigma_{c}})d\mathscr{C}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

does not have a closed form.
 In fact, one rarely sees equation (59) used as the sole basis for estimating
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 (Unlike in the probit model, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 are separately identified in (56), although that does not mean they are
 easy to estimate.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we estimate the model in (58) under the full set of RE logit assumptions
 (or even if we just use equation (56)), the partial effects at interesting
 values of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are easier to obtain than the APEs: we just use differences or derivatives
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})$
\end_inset

 with respect to elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 and plug in interesting values of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, which can be determined because 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{c}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Further, we can apply assumption (54), and then we can estimate the uncondition
al mean and variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as in the probit case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The real advantage of specifying equation (58) in place of (50) is that
 under the logit specification, we can obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent, asymptotically normal estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 without 
\emph on
any
\emph default
 assumptions on 
\begin_inset Formula $D(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, provided, of course, that each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is time varying.
 In addition to assumption (58), we maintain the strict exogeneity assumption
 (49) and the conditional independence assumption (51).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How can we allow 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be arbitrarily related in the unobserved effects logit model? In the
 linear case, we used the FE or FD transformation to eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 from the estimating equation.
 It turns out that a similar strategy works in the logit case, although
 the arument is more subtle.
 What we do is find the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}\equiv(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}\equiv\sum_{t=1}^{T}y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 It turns out that this conditional distribution does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, so that it is also the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we can use standard CMLE methods to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 (The fact that we can find a conditional distribution that does not depend
 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a feature of the logit functional form.
 Unfortunately, the same argument does not work for the unobserved effects
 probit model.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, consider the 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

 case, where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}$
\end_inset

 takes a value in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{0,1,2\}$
\end_inset

.
 Intuitively, the conditional distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},y_{i2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot be informative for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=2$
\end_inset

 because these values completely determine the outcome of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 However, for 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=1$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{i2}=1|x_{i}c_{i},n_{i}=1) & = & \frac{P(y_{i2}=1,n_{i}=1|x_{i},c_{i})}{P(n_{i}=1|x_{i},c_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{P(y_{i2}=1|x_{i},c_{i})P(y_{i1}=0|x_{i},c_{i})}{\left\{ P(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=1|x_{i},c_{i})+P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0|x_{i},c_{i})\right\} }\\
 & = & \frac{\Lambda(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]}{\left\{ \left[1-\Lambda(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\Lambda(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})+\Lambda(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\right\} }\\
 & = & \frac{\nicefrac{\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})}{\left[1+\exp(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\left[1+\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]}}{\nicefrac{\exp(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})+\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})}{\left[1+\exp(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\left[1+\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]}}\\
 & = & \frac{\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})}{\exp(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})+\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta)}{\exp(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta)+\exp(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta)}\\
 & = & \frac{\exp(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta}{1+\exp(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}}\\
 & = & \Lambda\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Similarly, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i1}=1|x_{i},c_{i},n_{i}=1)=\Lambda\left[-(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta\right]=1-\Lambda\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The conditional log likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\beta)=1\left[n_{i}=1\right]\left(w_{i}\log\Lambda\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta\right]\right)+(1-w_{i})\log\left\{ 1-\Lambda\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})^{\prime}\beta\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0)$
\end_inset

.
 The CMLE is obtained by maximizing the sum of the 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\beta)$
\end_inset

 across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 The indicator function 
\begin_inset Formula $1\left[n_{i}=1\right]$
\end_inset

 selects out the observations for which 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=1$
\end_inset

; as stated earlier, observations for which 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=2$
\end_inset

 do not contribute to the log likelihood.
 Interestingly, equation (60) is just a standard cross sectional logit of
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i2}-x_{i1})$
\end_inset

 using the observations for which 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 (This approach is analogous to differencing in the linear case with 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The CMLE from equation (60) is often called the 
\series bold
fixed effects logit estimator
\series default
 and sometimes called the 
\series bold
conditional logit estimator
\series default
.
 We must emphasize that the FE logit estimator does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 arise by treating the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as parameters to be estimated along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 that is obtained by maximizing the log likelihood over 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 has probability limit 
\begin_inset Formula $2\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes the CMLE is described as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

conditioning on the unobserved effects in the sample.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The description is misleading.
 What we have done is found a conditional density, which describes the subpopula
tion with 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, that depends only on observable data and the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 One should not expend energy worrying about the loss of observations for
 which 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}=y_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $D(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is unrestricted, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is free to vary as much as required to make 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 arbitrarily close to one (if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=1,\ t=1,2$
\end_inset

) or arbitrarily close to zero (if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=0,\ t=1,2$
\end_inset

).
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 does not change across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 can adjust to make both observed outcomes occur with certainty.
 Clearly, such data points contain no information for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and so they 
\emph on
should
\emph default
 drop out of the estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For general 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 the log likelihood is more complicated, but it is tractable.
 First,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=y_{1},\ldots,y_{iT}=y_{T}|x_{i},c_{i},n_{i}=n)=\frac{P(y_{i1}=y_{1},\ldots,y_{iT}=y_{T}|x_{i},c_{i})}{P(n_{i}=n|x_{i},c_{i})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the numerator factors as 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i1}=y_{1}|x_{i},c_{i})\cdots P(y_{iT}=y_{t}|x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 by the conditional independence assumption.
 The denominator is the complicated part, but it is easy to describe: 
\begin_inset Formula $P(n_{i}=n|x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the sum of the probabilities of all possible outcomes of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}=n$
\end_inset

.
 Using the specific form of the logit function, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\beta)=\log\left\{ \exp\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}y_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\beta\right)\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{a\in R_{i}}}\exp\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}a_{t}x_{it}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]^{-1}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{T}$
\end_inset

 defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a\in\mathbb{R}^{T}:a_{t}\in\{0,1\}\ \mbox{and}\ \sum_{t=1}^{T}a_{t}=n_{i}\}$
\end_inset

.
 The log likelihood summed across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 can be used to obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

- asymptotically normal estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and all inference follows form conditional MLE theory.
 Observations for which equation (61) is zero or unity, and which therefore
 do not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, drop out of 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\beta)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The FE logit estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 immediately gives us the effect of each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on the log-odds ration, 
\begin_inset Formula $\log\left\{ \nicefrac{\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})}{\left[1-\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}\right]}\right\} =x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Unfortunately, we cannot estimate the partial effects on the response probabili
ties unless we plug in a value for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Because the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unrestricted, in particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

 is not necessarily zero, it is hard to know what to plug in for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, we cannot estimate APEs, as doing so would require finding
 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\Lambda(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]$
\end_inset

, a task that apparently requires specifying a distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The conditional logit approach also has the drawback of apparently requiring
 the conditional independence assumption (51) for consistency.
 If we are willing to make the normality assumption (54), the probit approach
 allows unrestricted serial dependence in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 even after conditioning on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This possibility may be especially important when several time periods
 are available.
\end_layout

